WEBVTT

00:06.440 --> 00:08.680
So let's start with the first function is null.

00:08.960 --> 00:12.920
Is null going to go and replace a null with a specific value?

00:12.960 --> 00:15.280
Now the syntax of the is null is very simple.

00:15.280 --> 00:19.440
We're going to use the keyword isnull and it accepts two arguments.

00:19.440 --> 00:22.880
First the value and then the second the replacement value.

00:22.880 --> 00:23.960
So let's have an example.

00:23.960 --> 00:28.120
We can go and use the isnull for the column called shipping address.

00:28.120 --> 00:30.080
So we are checking the nulls inside it.

00:30.080 --> 00:35.600
And if SQL encounters any null it's going to go and replace it with the value unknown.

00:35.600 --> 00:38.600
So this can be like a default value for the nulls.

00:38.600 --> 00:40.480
So the first value is a column.

00:40.480 --> 00:42.640
And the second value is like static.

00:42.640 --> 00:46.080
Always going to be the unknown if we find any nulls.

00:46.120 --> 00:50.160
Now of course in other scenarios we don't want to have it always like the unknown.

00:50.200 --> 00:54.040
We would like to use another column to help the first one.

00:54.040 --> 00:55.600
So let's have this scenario.

00:55.600 --> 00:59.120
So now with this syntax we are checking the values of the shipping address.

00:59.120 --> 01:04.650
And if we find any nulls it's going to get the replacement from the billing address.

01:04.650 --> 01:06.770
So here in this example we have two columns.

01:06.770 --> 01:08.890
We don't have here any static value.

01:08.930 --> 01:13.810
We will get the values of the billing address only if the shipping address is null.

01:13.810 --> 01:17.610
So we are replacing the nulls using the help of other column.

01:17.770 --> 01:23.250
And in the first scenario we are replacing the nulls with a static value, the default value.

01:23.250 --> 01:26.610
So let's have a very simple example in order to learn how this works.

01:26.610 --> 01:29.930
So what we are doing we are checking whether the value is null.

01:29.970 --> 01:34.090
If it's yes, then we're going to go and get the value from the replacement.

01:34.090 --> 01:37.570
And if the value is not null then show the value itself.

01:37.570 --> 01:38.850
So we have the following example.

01:38.850 --> 01:41.690
We are going to check the values from the shipping address.

01:41.930 --> 01:46.130
And if there is nulls then go replace it with the default value na.

01:46.170 --> 01:49.290
So let's see how SQL going to go and execute this very simple example.

01:49.290 --> 01:50.370
We have two orders.

01:50.570 --> 01:53.970
The first order we are checking the shipment address is.

01:53.970 --> 01:56.370
The value of this address is null.

01:56.490 --> 01:58.450
Well no we have a value a.

01:58.650 --> 02:02.290
So that's why SQL going to go and return the same value.

02:02.290 --> 02:05.130
So in the outputs we will get a.

02:05.170 --> 02:07.910
So if it's not null, it's going to return the same value.

02:07.910 --> 02:09.910
So now it's going to move to the second order.

02:09.910 --> 02:12.590
And here we have the shipment address as a null.

02:12.590 --> 02:13.790
So what can happen here.

02:13.790 --> 02:17.510
If the value is null then we're going to get the replacement value.

02:17.710 --> 02:20.470
So what is the replacement value is the na.

02:20.910 --> 02:24.150
So that's why in the output we will not get a null.

02:24.150 --> 02:26.110
We will get the n a.

02:26.230 --> 02:28.150
So if you check the result what happens.

02:28.150 --> 02:30.910
We're going to get the addresses from the shipping address.

02:30.910 --> 02:32.470
But only if we have a null.

02:32.470 --> 02:34.270
We will get like default value.

02:34.270 --> 02:39.310
It's very important to understand if you are using the default value in the output, you will never

02:39.350 --> 02:40.230
get a null.

02:40.270 --> 02:40.550
All right.

02:40.550 --> 02:45.030
So let's have another example for the second scenario where we are not using a default value.

02:45.070 --> 02:46.430
We are using a column.

02:46.430 --> 02:48.950
So we have a supportive column that's going to be checked.

02:49.150 --> 02:53.070
So in this scenario we are saying is null shipping address and billing address.

02:53.070 --> 02:54.270
So we have two columns.

02:54.310 --> 02:56.350
And of course the logic can be the same.

02:56.350 --> 02:56.590
Right.

02:56.590 --> 02:58.470
So we are checking only once.

02:58.510 --> 03:00.670
Let's see how SQL can execute this example.

03:00.670 --> 03:02.350
We have this time three orders.

03:02.350 --> 03:05.630
And we have addresses from the shipments and as well from billing.

03:05.630 --> 03:11.480
So now SQL is always focusing on the shipping address since it is the first column, so we are not checking

03:11.480 --> 03:12.640
the billing address at all.

03:12.960 --> 03:14.520
So it starts with the first order.

03:14.640 --> 03:15.400
Is it null?

03:15.440 --> 03:16.160
Well, no.

03:16.200 --> 03:20.080
We have the value a so we will get it as well in the output.

03:20.080 --> 03:23.760
And SQL will not get anything from the billing address.

03:23.760 --> 03:24.960
So we will get a.

03:25.000 --> 03:26.520
So that's it for the first order.

03:26.560 --> 03:28.520
Now it's going to go to the second order.

03:28.520 --> 03:30.440
And this time we're going to have a null.

03:30.480 --> 03:36.320
So now in the rule we are saying if the shipping address is a null go get the value from the billing

03:36.360 --> 03:36.960
address.

03:37.000 --> 03:39.800
So this time we're going to go to the replacement right.

03:39.920 --> 03:45.720
So we will get the value see in the output because the shipping address is the null.

03:45.760 --> 03:46.960
Now let's move to the third row.

03:47.000 --> 03:48.880
As you can see here we have again a null.

03:48.880 --> 03:52.800
So SQL can go and get the value from the billing address.

03:52.800 --> 03:56.680
But here in this scenario the billing address is as well null.

03:56.720 --> 04:00.720
That's why we will get the value null in the output.

04:00.760 --> 04:06.040
So as you can see having the replacement values from a column, there is no guarantee that there will

04:06.040 --> 04:10.360
be always a value like here in the third order it is a null.

04:10.400 --> 04:13.210
That's why we will get null as well in the output.

04:13.250 --> 04:19.170
So if you think you are using is null to replace all the nulls by having two columns, you might end

04:19.170 --> 04:21.610
up as well having a null in the output.

04:21.650 --> 04:23.570
If the replacement having nulls.

04:23.570 --> 04:29.010
So if you want to make sure you don't get any nulls in the output, you have to go and use a static

04:29.010 --> 04:29.530
value.

04:29.530 --> 04:32.370
So this is how SQL executes the is null.

04:36.290 --> 04:36.730
All right.

04:36.730 --> 04:38.290
So what is coalesce.

04:38.330 --> 04:43.050
Coalesce is going to go and return the first non-null value from a list.

04:43.050 --> 04:43.370
All right.

04:43.370 --> 04:47.450
So now the syntax of that coalesce is way better than the is null here.

04:47.450 --> 04:50.650
It accepts like a list of many values.

04:50.650 --> 04:52.970
So here for example we have value 123.

04:53.010 --> 04:55.930
You can add four five as much as you want.

04:55.970 --> 04:59.170
So we are creating here a list of values to be checked.

04:59.170 --> 05:04.650
So for example we still can use it like the is null where we have the shipping address, where we replace

05:04.650 --> 05:07.290
the null with static value, the unknown.

05:07.290 --> 05:11.530
Or as we learned we can go and use two columns shipping address and the billing address.

05:11.530 --> 05:14.300
So so far it's like the same use cases as the is null.

05:14.340 --> 05:17.420
But now, of course, the coalesce is not only limited to two.

05:17.460 --> 05:19.260
We can go and use three.

05:19.260 --> 05:21.380
So we are saying go check the shipping address.

05:21.380 --> 05:24.100
If it's null, then go check the billing address.

05:24.100 --> 05:29.460
If it's as well null then use at the end the default value, the static one, the unknown.

05:29.460 --> 05:32.940
So as you can see, we can use more than two values with the coalesce.

05:32.980 --> 05:33.220
Okay.

05:33.260 --> 05:36.220
So now let's understand that coalesce and how this works.

05:36.500 --> 05:39.460
Now the workflow is something similar to the Isnull.

05:39.460 --> 05:43.460
So in this example we have two columns shipping address and the billing address.

05:43.820 --> 05:45.140
Again consider it as a list.

05:45.140 --> 05:47.820
And it's going to start checking from left to right.

05:47.820 --> 05:50.820
So it's going to check the first value from the shipping address.

05:51.140 --> 05:52.020
Whether it's null.

05:52.060 --> 05:55.820
If no it's not null then we're going to go and get the value one.

05:55.820 --> 05:58.420
So we will get the value from the shipping address.

05:58.420 --> 06:02.580
And if yes it is null then it's going to go and get the value two.

06:02.620 --> 06:04.900
So we're going to get the value from the shipping address.

06:04.900 --> 06:06.260
Now we have the similar data.

06:06.260 --> 06:07.300
We have three orders.

06:07.300 --> 06:08.940
Let's see how SQL can execute it.

06:08.940 --> 06:12.780
So it's going to start with the first row and it's going to focus on the shipping address.

06:12.780 --> 06:14.700
So here the value is not null.

06:14.700 --> 06:16.220
So we have it as an A.

06:16.560 --> 06:19.320
So that's why we will get the value one.

06:19.320 --> 06:24.240
So we will get the value from the shipping address and nothing else going to be checked.

06:24.280 --> 06:25.920
Now moving on to the second row.

06:25.960 --> 06:27.960
This time the shipping address is null.

06:28.280 --> 06:32.400
So SQL going to go and get the value from the second column.

06:32.400 --> 06:34.040
And it's going to be the C right.

06:34.600 --> 06:37.000
So in the output we will get C.

06:37.040 --> 06:39.480
Now to the last example we have it as a null.

06:39.480 --> 06:42.560
And the SQL is going to go and get the value from the second column.

06:42.840 --> 06:46.960
And this time we're going to get as well a null like that is null function.

06:47.320 --> 06:51.280
So at the results we are getting exactly the same result as is null.

06:51.320 --> 06:55.000
So for this scenario it doesn't matter whether you use Isnull or coalesce.

06:55.040 --> 06:59.760
So now of course we are still not happy with that because I don't want to see any nulls in the output,

06:59.960 --> 07:04.400
and I will still need to use the billing address instead of any static values.

07:04.400 --> 07:07.560
So I would like to have everything, the values from the billing address.

07:07.560 --> 07:13.440
And as well I would like to have at the end a default value so that I don't have any nulls in the output.

07:13.440 --> 07:14.560
So how are we going to solve it?

07:14.560 --> 07:20.970
So now we can use the power of the coalesce where we can include multiple values in one function.

07:20.970 --> 07:24.810
So what we're going to do, we're going to have the shipping address first then the billing address.

07:24.850 --> 07:27.530
And at the end we're going to have the default value.

07:27.530 --> 07:29.770
So we have now a list of three values.

07:29.770 --> 07:32.530
And of course our workflow is going to be a little bit bigger.

07:32.530 --> 07:35.330
So again here it's going to start from the left to the right.

07:35.330 --> 07:37.650
So first it's going to go and check the value one.

07:37.810 --> 07:41.730
If it is null then it's going to go as well checking the value two.

07:41.770 --> 07:45.410
And if the value two is as well null we will get the last value.

07:45.410 --> 07:46.850
It's going to be the value three.

07:46.850 --> 07:49.850
So now let's run the example again using the new coalesce.

07:49.850 --> 07:54.170
So the first thing we're going to go and check the first value which is the shipping address for the

07:54.170 --> 07:55.090
record number one.

07:55.090 --> 07:57.490
So now as you can see the value is not null.

07:57.490 --> 07:58.810
So we have here an A.

07:58.970 --> 08:00.130
So what's going to happen.

08:00.130 --> 08:03.210
We're going to get the value A as well in the output.

08:03.210 --> 08:06.010
So that means this one is going to be activated.

08:06.010 --> 08:08.130
And we will not check anything else.

08:08.130 --> 08:11.170
So that means in the output it's going to be like this.

08:11.170 --> 08:15.410
And the first value is returned and everything else will be ignored.

08:15.410 --> 08:17.250
So SQL will not check anything.

08:17.250 --> 08:22.100
So as you can see we are returning the first non-null value.

08:22.100 --> 08:23.900
So now let's move to the second order.

08:23.900 --> 08:25.580
Now we're going to check again the first value.

08:25.580 --> 08:26.380
Is it null?

08:26.420 --> 08:27.180
Well yes.

08:27.180 --> 08:28.820
As you can see we have here a null.

08:29.020 --> 08:34.100
So that means we're going to go and activate this path over here on the right side.

08:34.100 --> 08:38.940
So now SQL will not go blindly putting anything from the billing address in the results.

08:38.980 --> 08:40.860
First SQL has to check it.

08:40.860 --> 08:43.580
So SQL is going to check it whether it's null or not.

08:43.620 --> 08:46.540
This SQL is going to go and return it as well in the output.

08:46.540 --> 08:49.060
And we have activated this path.

08:49.060 --> 08:54.060
So SQL is returning the value two which is the value from the billing address.

08:54.060 --> 08:59.100
So now let's move to the third order SQL is going to go and check the shipping address.

08:59.140 --> 08:59.980
Is it null.

09:00.020 --> 09:01.420
Well yes it is null.

09:01.420 --> 09:06.140
So that's why SQL is going to go and start checking the second value.

09:06.140 --> 09:09.740
So this time SQL will not return the billing address value.

09:09.780 --> 09:10.860
Since it's null.

09:10.980 --> 09:13.900
It's going to go and return the third value.

09:13.900 --> 09:15.340
And what is the third value.

09:15.340 --> 09:17.820
It is our static value the na.

09:17.980 --> 09:23.180
So in the output we're going to get that na our default value.

09:23.180 --> 09:26.230
So with that, as you can see in the output, we will not get any nulls.

09:26.230 --> 09:29.870
We are using the default value and as well multiple columns.

09:29.870 --> 09:35.390
So if you check the output, it's always the first priority to check the values from the first column

09:35.390 --> 09:36.510
the shipping address.

09:36.510 --> 09:40.310
If it's null, then the second priority is going to be the billing address.

09:40.310 --> 09:44.710
If it's null, then the last priority it's going to be the default value.

09:44.710 --> 09:50.950
So as you can see SQL is checking the values from left to right, and it stops immediately once it encounters

09:51.150 --> 09:54.790
the first not null value and return it in the results.

09:54.790 --> 09:56.830
So this is how the Cavaliers works.

10:00.710 --> 10:00.990
All right.

10:00.990 --> 10:05.230
So now let's have a quick summary about the differences between the Coalesce and Isnull.

10:05.230 --> 10:10.990
So as we learned the Isnull is limited only to two values where the coalesce is amazing, where you

10:10.990 --> 10:15.990
can have a list of multiple values, which is a great advantage compared to the Isnull.

10:16.030 --> 10:21.030
Now, if you are talking about the performance, the Isnull is faster than the coalesce.

10:21.030 --> 10:24.910
So if you want to optimize the performance of your query, then go with the Isnull.

10:24.950 --> 10:30.970
Now there is another problem with the that is not that we have different keywords for different databases.

10:30.970 --> 10:34.850
So for Microsoft SQL server we use the Isnull as we learned.

10:34.850 --> 10:37.450
But in Oracle they have different implementations.

10:37.450 --> 10:42.770
They use the Nvl and other databases like MySQL you have Ifnull.

10:42.810 --> 10:45.130
And all those three functions are doing the same.

10:45.250 --> 10:47.810
But we have different implementations for different databases.

10:47.810 --> 10:52.010
But in the other hand the coalesce, it is available in all different databases.

10:52.010 --> 10:56.250
So here we have like an agreement or standards between the databases of using the coalesce.

10:56.250 --> 11:01.730
So here again this is a great advantage for the Coalesce because if you are writing like scripts and

11:01.730 --> 11:06.290
someday you want to migrate from one database to another, if you are using the coalesce, you don't

11:06.290 --> 11:07.450
have to change anything.

11:07.450 --> 11:13.250
But if you are using the Isnull, then you have to go and adjust your queries and scripts with the correct

11:13.250 --> 11:13.930
functions.

11:14.170 --> 11:20.490
That's why I tend always to use the coalesce and avoid using the Isnull only if it's really necessary

11:20.490 --> 11:22.450
that I have really bad performance.

11:22.450 --> 11:26.330
I go and try the isnull, but I usually stick with the coalesce.

11:26.370 --> 11:27.890
So that is my advice for you.

11:28.090 --> 11:30.210
Go with the coalesce and stick with the standard.
